## 1. Facts survey

### 1.1. Facts given in the task
- Review title: Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis for opportunistic infections in children with HIV infection
- Objective: To assess the effects of routinely administered cotrimoxazole on death and illness episodes in children 
with HIV infection, and in infants of HIV‐infected mothers.
- Background (key contextual points):
  - Most HIV-infected children live in low-income countries with limited ART access.
  - Prevention/early treatment of opportunistic infections is central to management.
  - Cotrimoxazole is inexpensive, broad-spectrum (including against PCP), and generally safe.
  - Early-life HIV diagnosis is complicated by maternal antibodies; prophylaxis based on maternal status is one 
strategy.
  - Routine prophylaxis in low-resource settings is challenging and may increase drug resistance.
- Selection criteria: Randomised or quasi-randomised trials comparing routinely administered cotrimoxazole vs placebo or
no treatment in:
  - Children <15 years with HIV infection, or
  - Children <18 months with HIV-infected mothers.
- Candidate studies provided (study_index, title, abstract):
  - 1: CHAP trial in Zambia; double-blind RCT; children 1–14 years with clinical HIV features; outcomes include 
mortality and adverse events; HR for death 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77); median follow-up 19 months; low PCP detection; 
similar adverse events in arms.
  - 2: Early chemoprophylaxis RCT in adults (Abidjan, Côte d'Ivoire); not pediatric.
  - 3: Chemoprophylaxis for PCP in adults with AIDS/Kaposi’s sarcoma; randomized, but adult.
  - 4: Case series of adult homosexual men with PCP and candidiasis; not RCT, not pediatric.
  - 5: Zimbabwe necropsy series in children ≤5 years; observational; not RCT of prophylaxis.
  - 6: Rwanda five-year pediatric cohort of HIV natural history; observational.
  - 7: Malawi prospective pediatric morbidity study; observational.
  - 8: Adult TB patients RCT of cotrimoxazole (Abidjan); not pediatric.
  - 9: Review/discussion on mother-to-child transmission; not an RCT.
  - 10: South African prospective study of PCP incidence/diagnostics in HIV-infected children; observational; notes low 
PCP with prophylaxis; not randomized.

### 1.2. Facts to look up
- None required from external sources to execute the provided systematic review pipeline because:
  - The librarian is supplied with the full candidate set (titles/abstracts).
