### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Daily co-trimoxazole prophylaxis in HIV-infected Zambian children (1–14 years) significantly reduced mortality versus 
placebo over a median of 19 months (HR 0.57, 95% CI 0.43–0.77; p=0.0002), with consistent benefit beyond 12 months (HR 
0.48, 95% CI 0.27–0.84). Severe adverse events were similar between groups.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
- Population: Children aged 1–14 years with clinical features of HIV infection in Zambia; 541 randomized; 7 HIV-negative
excluded. Setting characterized by high co-trimoxazole resistance; resource-poor.
- Design: Double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial; intention-to-treat; DSMC recommended early stopping (Oct 
2003). Median follow-up 19 months; subgroup >12 months (n=320).
- Intervention: Daily co-trimoxazole (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) prophylaxis (dose not specified in abstract) vs 
placebo.
- Primary outcomes: Mortality and adverse events possibly related to treatment.
- Mortality: 74/ (28%) co-trimoxazole vs 112/ (42%) placebo; HR 0.57 (95% CI 0.43–0.77), p=0.0002. Benefit persisted >12
months: HR 0.48 (95% CI 0.27–0.84); tests for heterogeneity: by follow-up duration p=0.60, by age p=0.82, by baseline 
CD4 p=0.36.
- Adverse events: Grade 3/4 events: 16 (6%) co-trimoxazole vs 18 (7%) placebo. Neutropenia (<0.5×10^9/L, one occasion): 
16 (6%) co-trimoxazole vs 7 (3%) placebo; p=0.06. Rash: 1 event (placebo).
- PCP: Pneumocystis carinii detected by immunofluorescence in 1/73 nasopharyngeal aspirates (placebo) among children 
with pneumonia.
- Conclusion (per abstract): Supports co-trimoxazole prophylaxis for HIV-infected children in resource-poor settings 
despite local resistance.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
Abstract does not report dosing specifics, hospitalization rates, or detailed illness episode counts; it focuses on 
mortality and adverse events. Counts/percentages and HRs reported here are quoted verbatim from the abstract.
