Final answer: ### 1. Task outcome (short version):
Routine cotrimoxazole in HIV-infected children substantially reduced mortality versus placebo (HR ≈0.57), with benefit 
persisting beyond 12 months; severe adverse events were similar, with a borderline increase in severe neutropenia. No 
direct evidence for infants of HIV-infected mothers or for nonfatal illness episodes in the abstracted data.

### 2. Task outcome (extremely detailed version):
One double-blind randomized placebo-controlled trial from Zambia (CHAP) enrolled 541 children aged 1–14 years with 
clinical features of HIV; 7 later determined HIV-negative were excluded from the HIV-infected analyses. Daily 
cotrimoxazole versus placebo reduced all-cause mortality with a substantial hazard reduction. Deaths were 74 vs 112 
(cotrimoxazole vs placebo). Among children followed >12 months, the mortality benefit persisted (HR 0.48, 95% CI 
0.27–0.84). Effects were consistent across age and baseline CD4 strata. Severe (grade 3/4) adverse events were similar 
(16 vs 18 events; ~6% vs 7%). Severe neutropenia occurred in 16 (6%) vs 7 (3%); p=0.06. Illness episodes were not 
systematically reported; outcomes centered on mortality and severe adverse events. The DSMC recommended early stopping 
(Oct 2003), which could overestimate effect sizes. Sequence generation/allocation concealment were not detailed in the 
abstract; dosing not specified. The study took place in a high-resistance, resource-poor setting, largely pre-ART, which
affects applicability to current ART-era programs. No included evidence directly addresses infants of HIV-infected 
mothers. Overall, internal validity is good, but breadth and modern applicability are limited.

### 3. Additional context (if relevant):
Findings align with historical WHO guidance supporting cotrimoxazole prophylaxis in pediatric HIV where bacterial 
infections are common. In contemporary ART-era programs, benefits may vary with local infection epidemiology and 
resistance; monitoring for hematologic toxicity and periodic policy re-evaluation are prudent.

